{*
   base.latte - the demo's layout, and the grandparent of the chain.

   It lives in /views/ because that is the only place aLatteX resolves a
   template reference from: SourceLoader takes a flat <name>.latte and looks for
   it in Evolution's configured view paths. A layout kept in the database cannot
   be extended, because there is nothing to resolve a name against.

   Note what is *not* here: a templatealias. A template record aliased "base"
   would make the CMS render this file directly for its documents, blocks and
   all. It is a layout, so it has none, and the CMS never renders it on its own
   - only as the parent of something that extends it.

   EVO tags below are deliberate, and they resolve however this layout was
   reached - from a template held in the database, or from one held in a file.
   The core skips its parser for a view-rendered document; aLatteX runs those
   passes itself so the two are the same. See docs/interop.md.
*}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{evoSetting('manager_language')}">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <title>{block title}{$pagetitle}{/block} - [(site_name)]</title>
</head>
<body class="alx alx-layout {block bodyClass}alx-base{/block}">

{{aLatteXDemoHeader}}

<p class="alx-crumbs">
    {block crumbs}
        <a href="[~1~]">Home</a> / {$pagetitle}
    {/block}
</p>

<main class="alx-main">
    {block content}
        <p class="alx-empty">
            base.latte on its own. A child template fills this in.
        </p>
    {/block}
</main>

<aside class="alx-aside">
    {block aside}
        <p>Default aside, from base.latte.</p>
    {/block}
</aside>

<footer class="alx-foot">
    {block foot}Rendered by base.latte{/block}
    - {{aLatteXDemoBadge}}
</footer>

{{aLatteXDemoFooter}}

</body>
</html>
